   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to animal\nresponse teams",
    "basePrintNo": "S6713",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6713-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd Art 26-B Art Head, add §411, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6713\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to animal\nresponse teams\n\n1.   \nPURPOSE:\n\nAdd Section 411 to the Article 26-B of the Agriculture and Markets Law\nto authorize the Commissioner to enter Into agreements with animal\nwelfare organizations, which can be deployed, if needed, in emergencies\nand disasters affecting animals as part of the state's emergency\nresponse. With the increased incidence of natural and other disasters\nthat threaten the lives of humans, wildlife, livestock and pets, the\nstate needs the authority to partner with and integrate credentialed\nnon-governmental agencies into its emergency response.  Also, amend the\ntitle of the Article to reflect the addition of the new Section 411.\n\n2.   \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nA new section 411 would be added to Article 26-B to provide the Commis-\nsioner with authority to enter into agreements with animal welfare\norganizations to support the prevention of, preparedness for, response\nto, and recovery from emergencies and disasters affecting animals in New\nYork state. The title of Article 26-B Wad also be amended to specify the\nemergency nature of the sections located therein.\n\n3.   \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nSection 410 of Article 26-B (Animal Response Teams) authorizes the\ncommissioner to establish response teams to address animal issues during\nemergencies and disasters and appointment of qualified volunteers to\nthose teams.\n\n4.   \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.10531 (Lupardo) was introduced and referred to the Committee on Agri-\nculture on 5/28/2020. This bill is substantially the same.\n\n5.   \nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nWITH THE INCREASED INCIDENCE OF NATURAL AND OTHER DISASTERS THAT THREAT-\nEN THE LIVES OF HUMANS, WILDLIFE, LIVESTOCK AND PETS, THE STATE NEEDS\nTHE AUTHORITY TO PARTNER WITH AND INTEGRATE CREDENTIALED NON-GOVERNMEN-\nTAL AGENCIES INTO ITS EMERGENCY RESPONSE. THE ABSENCE OF STATUTORY\nAUTHORITY FOR THE DEPARTMENT TO ENTER INTO AGREEMENTS WITH ANIMAL\nWELFARE ORGANIZATIONS TO ASSIST WHEN NEEDED THREATENS THE STATE'S, ABIL-\nITY TO OBTAIN THE ASSISTANCE OF AND INTEGRATE THOSE ORGANIZATIONS'\nEFFORTS INTO THE STATE'S EMERGENCY MANAGEMENT RESPONSE.\n\n6.   \nPOSSIBLE OBJECTIONS:\n\nTHERE IS NO KNOWN OPPOSITION.\n\n7.   \nOTHER STATE AGENCIES INTERESTED:\n\nDHSES, DIVISION OF STATE POLICE, DEC\n8.   \nKNOWN POSITION OF OTHERS:\n\nUNKNOWN.\n\n9.   \nBUDGET:\n\nNONE IN CONNECTION WITH OBTAINING AUTHORITY TO CONTRACT WITH, ANIMAL\nWELFARE ORGANIZATIONS. IN NEGOTIATING SUCH AGREEMENTS, THE STATE MAY BE\nASKED TO INDEMNIFY THE ORGANIZATION SHOULD CLAIMS BE ASSERTED IN\nCONNECTION WITH ITS PARTICIPATION IN THE EMERGENCY RESPONSE, GIVING RISE\nTO POSSIBLE CONTINGENT LIABILITIES.\n\n10.   \nLOCAL IMPACT:\n\nTHIS BILL WOULD NOT IMPOSE A MANDATE ON A COUNTY, CITY, TOWN, VILLAGE,\nSCHOOL DISTRICT OR SPECIAL DISTRICT\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
